// Tests for src/c3/annex_g.c3 (port of src/aro/annex_g.zig), plan 10 agent 10.5.
//
// Provenance of the expected values:
//   - VECTORS: OBSERVED by running src/aro/annex_g.zig, copied verbatim, under
//     the Zig 0.17.0 binary at /home/user/tools/zig/ziglang/zig (x86_64-linux,
//     `zig run`, Debug and ReleaseSafe gave identical output) on a standalone
//     probe program. Each case is given as f64 literals, @floatCast to T, then
//     complexFloatMul/complexFloatDiv(T, a, b, c, d); the table records the bits
//     of the four inputs after the cast and of the two results. The probe source
//     is reproduced at the bottom of this file.
//   - The three "embedded_zig_*" tests are the `test` blocks of annex_g.zig
//     (source-derived; predicates only, as in Zig).
//   - The "native_reference_*" tests check a local native-float reference port of
//     annex_g.zig (module annex_g_tests::nref, with exact ports of Zig 0.17.0
//     std.math.ldexp / ilogb on bits) against the f16/f32/f64 vectors. They
//     exercise the algorithm structure independently of the KeyFloat arithmetic
//     that plan 10 agents 10.1/10.2 implement.
//
// Reference quirks pinned by the vectors (match them, do not "fix" them):
//   - Zig 0.17.0 std.math.ldexp(+-0, n) is NOT zero when n exceeds the format's
//     zero "exponent" offset (n > mant_bits + 1 for f16/f32/f64, n > 64 for f80,
//     n > 113 for f128): the zero-exponent normalisation uses @clz(0) and builds
//     a garbage normal number (f80: an unnormal, which classifies as NaN). Annex G
//     divides by scalbn(.., -denom_logb), so dividing by a tiny divisor turns an
//     exact-zero numerator part into a nonzero/NaN result, e.g.
//     f16 (1 + 0i) / (1e-4 + 0i) = 0x70e2 + 0xbdf5 i (imag -1.49, not 0) and
//     f64 (1 + 0i) / (2^-1074 + 0i) = inf - inf i, f80 gives NaN + NaN i.
//   - complexFloatMul's third branch ("recover infinities from overflow") never
//     sets recalc, so x and y stay NaN (f64 (1e308 + NaN i) * (1e308 + 1i)).
//   - NaN signs follow the width's hardware/compiler-rt rules (SSE / x87
//     negative default NaN; f128 compiler-rt positive default NaN and `a - NaN`
//     flips the NaN's sign, see float_value.c3).
//
// Because the KeyFloat arithmetic (float_soft_narrow.c3, float_soft_wide.c3) was
// still a stage-0 stub when this file was written, the target is tagged
// "manual"; the integrator should drop the tag once 10.1/10.2 have landed.
module annex_g_tests;
import std::io;
import aro::value_interner, aro::float_value, aro::annex_g;

enum Op : char
{
	MUL,
	DIV,
}

struct Vec
{
	Op op;
	KeyFloatTag tag;
	uint128 a;
	uint128 b;
	uint128 c;
	uint128 d;
	uint128 x;
	uint128 y;
}

